<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shirkuh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1169">1169</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shirkuh was a Kurdish general in the service of the Zengid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>, and the uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. He led expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, dying in 1169. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> note that Saladin succeeded him as vizier, the start of his rise to power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shirkuh.jpg" alt="Shirkuh" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amaury1A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Asad al-Dīn Shīrkūh was a Kurdish commander who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>. He was the uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, and his efforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were a key factor in establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> there. His name may mean the mountain lion, and his honorific Asad ad-Din means the lion of faith. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers called him Siraconus.</p><p>He came from a Kurdish village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> near the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a>, the son of the Kurdish chief Shadhi ibn Marwan and brother of Najm ad-Din Ayyub, ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>. When the last Shaddadid was deposed in Dvin in 1130 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and then to Tikrit, where Shadhi was made governor. In 1138 Shirkuh killed a man he was quarrelling with in Tikrit, and the brothers were exiled; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> was supposedly born the night they left. They entered Zengid service, and Shirkuh received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, ar-Rahba and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>. In 1154 the brothers negotiated the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to Nur ad-Din.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1163 Shawar asked Nur ad-Din to intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in his dispute with Dirgham over the Fatimid vizierate. Nur ad-Din sent Shirkuh, with a grant of 200,000 dinars and 2,000 soldiers chosen from his regiments, in the first of three ventures into Egypt; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> went as his advisor. Shawar was restored and Dirgham killed, but Shawar quarrelled with Shirkuh and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a>, who besieged Shirkuh at Bilbeis in 1164. Nur ad-Din meanwhile attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and almost captured the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><p>The Fatimid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">al-Adid</a> invited Shirkuh back in 1167 to help defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Shawar again allied with Amalric, who besieged Shirkuh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> until he agreed to leave. A Crusader garrison stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Amalric allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Shawar switched sides to Shirkuh, and after a pitched battle the Crusaders retreated. In January 1169 Shirkuh entered Cairo, was welcomed by al-Adid, and had Shawar executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shirkuh accepted the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> but died two months later, in March 1169, of a severe quinsy, according to Baha ad-Din ibn Shaddad, who says he was given to rich meats and suffered many bouts of indigestion. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> succeeded him as vizier and from 1174 also succeeded Nur ad-Din, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Some historians hold that Shirkuh's death helped Saladin consolidate his position as undisputed head of the Ayyubid family. In 1179 Saladin gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> to Shirkuh's son Muhammad, whose descendants ruled it until 1263.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shirkuh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saladin's path to power began in March 1169, when his uncle <b>Shirkuh</b>, the Zengid general who had taken control of Fatimid Egypt, died suddenly and Saladin was appointed vizier in his place.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1163 Nur ad-Din contested Egypt with Amalric of Jerusalem: <b>Shirkuh</b>, with his nephew Saladin, won the Battle of al-Babein in 1167, relieved Cairo in 1168 and in 1169 killed the vizier Shawar and seized Egypt for Nur ad-Din.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1179 Saladin granted Homs to his cousin Nasir al-Din Muhammad, son of <b>Shirkuh</b>, the uncle who had conquered Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-homs">Ayyubid Emirate of Homs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrarat">Ayrarat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.92,44.72&amp;year=1159" aria-label="Ayrarat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5651 735 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shirkuh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6101 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6101 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.92,44.72&amp;year=1159">What was here in 1159? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
